<p>Wall Street economists had expected the preliminary estimate of first quarter GDP to show a 6.0 percent rise, the price index to be up 2.7 percent and the implicit deflator to rise 2.3 percent.</p>
<p>Commerce provides three estimates of GDP per quarter. The first is the advance (A) estimate, the second is the preliminary (P) estimate, and the last is the final (F) estimate.</p>
<p>The 5.8 percent jump in the first quarter preliminary GDP was the biggest since fourth quarter 1987's rise of 6.0 percent.  </p>
<p>The 2.8 percent gain in the first quarter GDP price index was the biggest rise since the first quarter of 1995 when it advanced at a rate of 3.3 percent.</p>
<p>First quarter business inventories rose at the fastest rate since the first quarter of 1995, when they increased $53.7 billion.</p>
<p>The department said the upward revision to real GDP in the first quarter reflects upward revisions to inventory investment and to exports that more than offset downward revisions to consumer spending.</p>
